// llhttp -- Node's HTTP/1.1 parser, bound for sysl.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       llhttp { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/llhttp", version = "0.1.0" }
//     }
//
// **Nothing has to be installed.** llhttp is three C files and a header, all vendored here, so
// there is no `@link`, no `pkg_config` requirement and no library for the machine to be missing.
// The big one, `llhttp.c`, is generated by llhttp's own build from a TypeScript description of the
// state machine; it is copied from the `release/v9.4.3` tag unmodified and is never edited here.

package {
  name    = "llhttp"
  version = "0.1.0"

  // **The compiler this was written against.** The floor is a fact rather than a reconstruction:
  // nothing here has been built with an older release. What it certainly needs is a closure written
  // as an argument opening its own indented block, which arrived in 0.0.76 -- every callback in the
  // module above is registered that way.
  sysl = "0.0.89"
}

// **`heap`, because a parser is a `&Parser`.** llhttp hands a callback the parser it was given and
// nothing else, so the sysl value the callback belongs to has to be findable from that address --
// which means the parser's storage has to stay where it was put. A `&T` box is what does not move.
//
// **llhttp itself allocates nothing**: `llhttp_alloc` and `llhttp_free` are the only calls that
// would, and neither is bound. Every byte the parser writes goes into storage this package owns.
//
// **It needs a libc, and that is worth saying plainly rather than leaving to a link error.**
// `api.c` includes `<stdio.h>`, `<stdlib.h>` and `<string.h>` -- the first for a debug printer that
// is compiled whether or not it is called, the second for the two allocating calls, the third for
// one `memset`. So this builds for any target with a C library, which is the interesting set: the
// Pico SDK carries newlib, and so do Zephyr and FreeRTOS applications. It does not build for a bare
// `thumb-freestanding`, where clang supplies `stdint.h` and `stddef.h` and none of those three.
requires {
  heap = true
}
